<com:TContent ID="body" >

<h1>TFileUpload</h1>
<com:DocLink ClassPath="Prado.Web.UI.WebControls.TFileUpload" />

<p class="block-content">
<tt>TFileUpload</tt> displays a file upload field on a Web page. Upon postback, the text entered into the field will be treated as the (local) name of the file that is uploaded to the server.
</p>
<p class="block-content">
<tt>TFileUpload</tt> raises an <tt>OnFileUpload</tt> event when it is post back. The property <tt>HasFile</tt> indicates whether the file upload is successful or not. If successful, the uploaded file may be saved on the server by calling <tt>saveAs()</tt> method.
</p>
<p class="block-content">
The following properties give the information about the uploaded file:
</p>
<ul id="u1" class="block-content">
  <li><tt>FileName</tt> - the original client-side file name without directory information.</li>
  <li><tt>FileType</tt> - the MIME type of the uploaded file.</li>
  <li><tt>FileSize</tt> - the file size in bytes.</li>
  <li><tt>LocalName</tt> - the absolute file path of the uploaded file on the server. Note, this file will be deleted after the current page request is completed. Call <tt>saveAs()</tt> to save the uploaded file.</li>
</ul>
<p class="block-content">
If the file upload is unsuccessful, the property <tt>ErrorCode</tt> gives the error code describing the cause of failure. See <a href="https://www.php.net/manual/en/features.file-upload.errors.php">PHP documentation</a> for a complete explanation of the possible error codes.
</p>

<h2>Saving the file</h2>

<p class="block-content">
<tt>LocalName</tt> points at a temporary file that PHP deletes when the request ends.
A handler that needs to keep the upload calls <tt>saveAs()</tt> with a destination path.
The method moves the temporary file by default. Passing false as its second argument
copies it instead and leaves the temporary file in place.
</p>

<com:TTextHighlighter Language="php" CssClass="source block-content">
public function fileUploaded($sender, $param)
{
    if ($sender-&gt;HasFile) {
        $sender-&gt;saveAs($this-&gt;Application-&gt;RuntimePath . '/' . $sender-&gt;FileName);
    }
}
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<h2>The form encoding</h2>

<p class="block-content">
A browser sends files only when the form encoding is <tt>multipart/form-data</tt>. The
control sets that encoding on the page's form during pre-render, so a template needs no
change of its own. The control does the same during a callback by setting the attribute
on the client.
</p>

<h2>Size limits</h2>

<p class="block-content">
<tt>MaxFileSize</tt> gives the browser an advisory limit in bytes, rendered as a
<tt>MAX_FILE_SIZE</tt> hidden field. It defaults to 1048576, which is one megabyte. The
value is advice rather than enforcement. The limits that actually hold are PHP's
<tt>upload_max_filesize</tt> and <tt>post_max_size</tt> settings, and a file that exceeds
them arrives with an <tt>ErrorCode</tt> instead of content.
</p>

<h2>Uploading several files at once</h2>
<com:SinceVersion Version="4.0.0" />

<p class="block-content">
Setting <tt>Multiple</tt> to true renders the HTML5 <tt>multiple</tt> attribute and adds
square brackets to the field name, so one field accepts a set of files.
</p>

<com:TTextHighlighter Language="prado" CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;com:TFileUpload ID="Upload" Multiple="true" OnFileUpload="filesUploaded" /&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<p class="block-content">
<tt>Files</tt> returns an array of <tt>TFileUploadItem</tt> objects, one for each file.
An item carries the same members as the control itself, so a loop reads
<tt>FileName</tt>, <tt>FileSize</tt>, <tt>FileType</tt>, <tt>LocalName</tt>,
<tt>ErrorCode</tt>, and <tt>HasFile</tt>, and calls <tt>saveAs()</tt>.
</p>

<com:TTextHighlighter Language="php" CssClass="source block-content">
public function filesUploaded($sender, $param)
{
    foreach ($sender-&gt;Files as $file) {
        if ($file-&gt;HasFile) {
            $file-&gt;saveAs($this-&gt;Application-&gt;RuntimePath . '/' . $file-&gt;FileName);
        }
    }
}
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<p class="block-content">
The single-file members still work when several files arrive. Each of them takes an
optional index that selects the file to report on, and the index defaults to zero.
</p>

<table class="parameters">
<tr><th>Member</th><th>Behavior with several files</th></tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>getHasFile($index)</tt></td>
	<td>Reports on one file. <tt>HasAllFiles</tt> reports on the whole set at once.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>getFileName($index)</tt>, <tt>getFileSize($index)</tt>, <tt>getFileType($index)</tt></td>
	<td>Report on one file.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>saveAs($path, $delete, $index)</tt></td>
	<td>Saves one file.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>ValidationPropertyValue</tt></td>
	<td>Returns the original file names as a comma separated list, so a validator sees every file.</td>
</tr>
</table>

<p class="block-content">
<tt>OnFileUpload</tt> fires once for the request, whether one file arrived or several,
and whether the transfers succeeded or failed.
</p>

